data.content:
The lightest option is .NET's built-in **`TimeProvider`** (available since .NET 8). No DI container needed — just accept it as an optional constructor parameter:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use the built-in fake:

```csharp
var fake = new Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is lightest:**
- `TimeProvider` is in the BCL — zero production dependencies
- Default parameter means existing callers don't break
- Test project just needs the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package
- No container, no interface to define yourself

